    {
      "category": "overview",
      "field": "description",
      "value": "The Architecture of Africa is very diverse, reflecting the continent's many climates, cultures, and histories. Traditional African architecture mixes building traditions, using a wide arrange of materials like mud, stone, and wood. Fractal scaling is commonly used in designs, making smaller parts of a structure mirror the overall design. This concept can be seen in village layouts and decorative elements.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0010",
        "chunk_title": "African Architecture",
        "chunk_order": 9,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "overview",
      "field": "examples",
      "value": "Historical stone structures like the great Zimbabwe walls show the long lasting and durable construction.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0010",
        "chunk_title": "African Architecture",
        "chunk_order": 9,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "overview",
      "field": "technique",
      "value": "Wattle and Daub is a technique used in many areas that take wooden frames covered in mud to create rounded structures that help to regulate the temperature inside.",
